  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 121 PM CDT Sun Sep 13 2026\n\nDeep moisture is increasing well across the area today with \nsatellite TPW values nearing 2\" along the coast. A weak shortwave \nrotating through under the ridge could be enough to help develop a \nfew sea breeze showers and storms this afternoon, along with some \ndaytime heating showers that have developed in the Victoria \nCrossroads. Moisture levels decrease some tomorrow, but could still \nsee a few showers/storms, mainly in the Victoria Crossroads. After \nthat, below normal moisture levels Tuesday and Wednesday should \nresult in little to no rain chance. Another wave of moisture moves \nin for Thursday with moderate rain chances that day. Much drier air \nmoves in for the weekend and could actually give us a bit of a break \nin minimum temps. More on that in a moment. \n\nNot much change in the summer heat through this period as what has \nbeen the warmest September on record for all three of our climate \nsites will continue to see above normal temperatures for the next \nseveral days. For reference, normal highs are as follows: CRP and \nVCT 90-91, LRD 93-94.  At least through Wednesday highs west will be \nabove 100 with mid and upper 90s east. Will have to see how much \nthis increasing moisture and cloud cover Thursday will do, but we \nshould drop at least a couple degrees off for a day or two - that \nsaid, the increased moisture would keep it feeling just as hot! Heat \nindex values through mid-week will top out around 110, and will \ncontinue to monitor for potential heat advisories daily. The drier \nair Friday/Saturday likely brings highs back above 100 west. As \npreviously mentioned, the drier air late in the week should allow \nfor a bit more efficient cooling, allowing low temperatures to drop \ninto the lower 70s. Until then though, upper 70s and lower 80s.",
